001=Automatic identifier or internal system control number.
002=The Accession Number is unique and sequential information that aims to classify and identify an item in a collection. It is understood as a mandatory description element and constitutes one of the main access points to the item.
010=Simple and direct nomenclature of the object (Ex: Chair, Coin, Painting).
020=Hierarchical category based on standardized classification systems, such as the Chenhall System for material culture.
030=Exact quantity of physical elements covered by this record (useful for undivided batches).
050=Transcribe the main title of the work (usually given by the author). If there is none, assign a generic one in brackets (Ex: [Precision Scale], Untitled; [River Landscape]).
060=Description based on the utilitarian function of the piece (Ex: Furniture, Numismatics, Tool).
070=Numeric or alphanumeric code associated with the smart tag attached to the piece for quick auditing.
100=A short text of up to two lines for quick identification of the object in search lists.
110=Text focused on the function and purpose of the object. What it is for, how it was used.
120=Objective description of shape, colors, structure, and decorative elements, from general to particular, from foreground to background. Do not make value judgments ("beautiful", "ugly").
200=Materials that make up the work/object, listed from preponderant to minority (Ex: Oil paint, canvas).
210=Materials of the structure supporting the work (Ex: Wooden chassis, marble base).
220=Form adopted to construct the work (Ex: Watercolor, Woodcut, Carpentry, Casting).
230=Measurements of the object in centimeters (cm). Separated by subfields: ^a (Height), ^b (Width), ^c (Depth), ^d (Diameter).
240=Measurements of the frame, base, or fixed display of the object.
250=Weight of the object, using grams (g) or kilograms (kg) as standard (Ex: 150 g, 2.5 kg).
300=Name of the person who created the piece. Standard: Last Name, First Name.
310=Company or factory responsible for the production of the commercial/industrial item.
320=Brief context about the author's life or the history of the factory (birth/death dates, place of foundation).
330=Standardize date entries: Exact: DD/MM/YYYY Approximate/Decade: ca. 1930 Period: 1970-1975 Century: 19th cent.
340=City, State, or Country where the piece was manufactured or created.
400=Name of the person, family, or institution that passed the item to the museum.
410=Formal date of legal entry of the object into the collection (DD/MM/YYYY).
420=Financial value paid for the piece, if acquired by purchase.
430=Controlled vocabulary (Purchase, Donation, Bequest, Collection, Transfer).
440=Trajectory of ownership of the object (previous owners) before reaching the last donor/seller.
500=Primary assessment (Good, Fair, Poor, Very Poor).
510=When the diagnosis was performed.
520=Safety rules (Ex: "Mandatory use of nitrile gloves", "Handling restricted to conservators").
530=Visible pathologies (Ex: Oxidation, craquelure, structural loss, biological attack).
540=Actions suggested by the conservator (Ex: Mechanical cleaning, quarantine).
550=Urgency classification (High, Medium, Low) for intervention on the object.
560=Records of past reports or restorations noticed on the piece (Ex: "Presence of old adhesive tape").
600=Indicate Yes or No.
610=Title of the grouping (Ex: Porcelain Tea Set).
620=Total number of independent items that form the set and receive an accession number.
630=Description of subordinate pieces that are not structurally detached (Ex: Dresser with 4 drawers).
700=Yes or No.
710=Total number of marks.
720=Recommended structure for detailed cataloging: ^a Type: Signature, Label, Plaque, Stamp. ^b Location: Mapping acronyms (Front: f.u.l. - front upper left; Back: b.c. - back center; Frame: f.u.c. - frame upper center; Tridimensional: f.f. - front face). ^l Language: Portuguese, English, French, etc. ^t Transcription / Content: The exact text in quotation marks.
730=Alternative/legacy fields for direct textual use of location (730), content (740), transcription (750), and additional observations about the inscriptions (760).
740=Alternative/legacy fields for direct textual use of location (730), content (740), transcription (750), and additional observations about the inscriptions (760).
750=Alternative/legacy fields for direct textual use of location (730), content (740), transcription (750), and additional observations about the inscriptions (760).
760=Alternative/legacy fields for direct textual use of location (730), content (740), transcription (750), and additional observations about the inscriptions (760).
800=Exact address in the museum (Ex: Technical Reserve 01, Rack B, Shelf 3).
810=Chronological record of places the piece has been.
820=Name of high-resolution image files linked to the piece for display in the system and internal use.
821=Compressed or watermarked versions intended for viewing in online catalogs (OPAC).
830=Context, social use, or historical relevance tied to the life of the piece outside the museum.
840=Bibliography or documents consulted to support the cataloging.
850=If the piece is outside the museum, record Loan information (850), Name of the Borrower/Institution (851), Contact (852), and who Authorized it (853).
851=If the piece is outside the museum, record Loan information (850), Name of the Borrower/Institution (851), Contact (852), and who Authorized it (853).
852=If the piece is outside the museum, record Loan information (850), Name of the Borrower/Institution (851), Contact (852), and who Authorized it (853).
853=If the piece is outside the museum, record Loan information (850), Name of the Borrower/Institution (851), Contact (852), and who Authorized it (853).
860=Details of the piece's protection. Policy number/company (860), Value assessment date (861), Financial note (862), and Author of the assessment (863).
861=Details of the piece's protection. Policy number/company (860), Value assessment date (861), Financial note (862), and Author of the assessment (863).
862=Details of the piece's protection. Policy number/company (860), Value assessment date (861), Financial note (862), and Author of the assessment (863).
863=Details of the piece's protection. Policy number/company (860), Value assessment date (861), Financial note (862), and Author of the assessment (863).
870=Any relevant data that does not fit into the previous fields.
880=Name of the cataloger/museologist responsible.
890=Systemic control of the Date of last edition (890), Person responsible for the edition (891), and post-physical movement auditing data (892 and 893).
891=Systemic control of the Date of last edition (890), Person responsible for the edition (891), and post-physical movement auditing data (892 and 893).
892=Systemic control of the Date of last edition (890), Person responsible for the edition (891), and post-physical movement auditing data (892 and 893).
893=Systemic control of the Date of last edition (890), Person responsible for the edition (891), and post-physical movement auditing data (892 and 893).
900=Person, heirs, or institution holding intellectual property.
910=Moral, Patrimonial, Image rights.
920=Public Domain, Copyright Protected, Orphan Work.
930=How the image of the object can be used (Ex: CC-BY, Strict Internal Use).
940=Year the work enters public domain (in Brazil, generally 70 years after the author's death).
950=Where the right was confirmed (Contracts, wills).
960=Specific rules required by the author/donor.
980=Database control field (automatic insertion/alteration by the software).